//? Title: Evaluate rolling-origin point and ensemble forecasts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

\abstract(
Compare persistence and seasonal-naive forecasts at six leads over a
chronological backtest. Score point errors and empirical predictive
distributions using information available at each origin.
)
\toc

Persistence repeats the last observation. Seasonal naive repeats the
corresponding observation 24 steps earlier. At each origin, add the 32
latest available errors for that model and lead to the point forecast.
A prior forecast contributes an error only once its target is observed.

The seasonal MASE denominator is the mean absolute seasonal difference
over the expanding observed prefix. sMAPE divides the absolute error
by the sum of the two magnitudes and multiplies by 200. A pair of zeros
contributes zero. Ensemble CRPS subtracts half the mean pairwise absolute
difference from the mean absolute deviation from the actual value. The
pair sum includes its zero diagonal and the implementation computes
it from adjacent gaps in the sorted draws.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/forecast_evaluation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<double> series;
  for (int t = 0; t < 1152; ++t) {
    const double phase = 2 * std::numbers::pi * t;
    series.push_back((1 + .12 * std::sin(phase / 192)) * std::sin(phase / 24)
                     + .35 * std::cos(phase / 12) + .2 * std::sin(phase / 73));
  }
  std::vector<std::int64_t> origins;
  for (int t = 960; t <= 1152-6; ++t) origins.push_back(t);
  const auto fit = n::naive_forecast_backtest(n::from_vector(series),origins,24,6,32);

  std::println("{} expanding-window origins with six leads.\n",origins.size());
  std::println("| Model | Mean MASE | Mean sMAPE (%) | Mean CRPS |");
  std::println("| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
  for (int model = 0; model < 2; ++model)
    std::println("| {} | {:.8g} | {:.8g} | {:.8g} |",model == 0 ? "Persistence" : "Seasonal naive",
        n::mean(n::select(fit.mase,0,model)).item(),n::mean(n::select(fit.smape,0,model)).item(),
        n::mean(n::select(fit.crps,0,model)).item());

  const std::string folder = "build/forecast_evaluation/";
  std::filesystem::create_directories(folder);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  n::save_npz(folder+"backtest.npz",{{"origins",n::from_vector(origins).to(n::DType::Int32)},
      {"actual",fit.actual},{"point",fit.point},{"scale",fit.scale},{"draws",fit.draws},
      {"mase",fit.mase},{"smape",fit.smape},{"crps",fit.crps}});

  auto figure = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,3).size(1080,360);
  const std::vector<double> lead{1,2,3,4,5,6};
  auto add = [&](const n::Tensor& measure,const std::string& label) {
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.lines(lead,n::select(measure,0,0).to_vector<double>(),"Persistence",{37,99,235})
        .lines(lead,n::select(measure,0,1).to_vector<double>(),"Seasonal naive",{191,88,34})
        .x_label("Forecast lead").y_label(label).title(label);
    figure.add(plot);
  };
  add(fit.mase,"MASE");
  add(fit.smape,"sMAPE (%)");
  add(fit.crps,"CRPS");
  figure.save_svg(folder+"scores.svg");
  std::filesystem::copy_file(folder+"scores.svg","figures/forecast_evaluation.svg",
                             std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
187 expanding-window origins with six leads.

| Model | Mean MASE | Mean sMAPE (%) | Mean CRPS |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Persistence | 2.5091543 | 90.958528 | 0.45412507 |
| Seasonal naive | 1.0353623 | 46.662572 | 0.20575495 |
\image(figures/forecast_evaluation.svg)
// result_end

Seasonal naive has lower average error on all three scores in this
fixture. The lead-wise plot shows how persistence degrades as the lead
approaches a quarter of the dominant seasonal cycle.

> References

- [Hyndman and Koehler, Another look at measures of forecast accuracy|https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijforecast.2006.03.001].
- [Gneiting and Raftery, Strictly Proper Scoring Rules, Prediction, and Estimation|https://sites.stat.washington.edu/raftery/Research/PDF/Gneiting2007jasa.pdf].
